#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198053 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198411 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d912b296-6fd0-57c2-84e8-ca0b56549756" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Scalar Function"}] :question/id #uuid "ea202ce3-07d4-5324-9ee4-f5617b430772" :question/math-academy-id "e-12477" :question/problem "For the transformation $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R,$ defined as\n\n![](images/55/55831f05bb13347baf6816e77b462b893906cead512607cc4caf796440bbc2d7.svg)\n\ncalculate $\\boldsymbol f'$ at ![](images/ab/abac407b29e870cfe7090d3a263109f10a122fe1de14abbd76baef6d7101b3d2.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by \n\n![](images/28/286bec34ba72c733264e8223150d56566d0f61797cb221bed0af0323c3e190c9.svg) \n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\\boldsymbol f$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x},$ then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol f$ at $\\mathbf{x},$ denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}),$ is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n![](images/33/33b42ebfc6c3f32ef92212f61a138080543e2e2981088f7b5760b23f7ac31c6c.svg)\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/35/354f034a96ffe5bde885644792f50636dc8cbde6f00d65159b0bdefdd836fa09.svg)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y)=2xy, & \\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y)={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n![](images/90/900488a105afb5aa10e9113db36cd9f2e834eac46300d7c0a521a7100ae0d88f.svg)\n\nand $\\boldsymbol{f}'$ at ![](images/ed/ed7c974c8fca89e98f542231572e558caf867b200843d2b222ccda4c68a1c33b.svg) is\n\n![](images/ab/abd152888f711ecd43a47e31269e40985178782591546e9d96912fd4c230afa0.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198054 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198412 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a818e04-786a-56b6-90cf-dcb573ccfa64" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Vector Function"}] :question/id #uuid "ea76d21f-eff8-5634-975f-2b5bd24caf54" :question/math-academy-id "e-12478" :question/problem "For the transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^3,$ defined as\n\n![](images/bb/bbb9192192b4d73f4993b3131a2b0bb1d0a0cddb77b754940d1b7684bfb08336.svg)\n\nthe total derivative $\\boldsymbol f'$ is given by \n\n![](images/2c/2cf231879630af578e33b201e5be0f65d019da9c49da7789f5921c05a8249c09.svg)\nFrom top to bottom, what are the missing entries?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by \n\n![](images/49/49c118b037a1589c6feb2c96afeaccb15fe905663393800638baff8cef327a3a.svg) \n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x},$ then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{x},$ denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}),$ is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n![](images/a5/a5ed3766e8ed6a2a5fd249078e244dfab19b1c20d55548c27a7053bd0130c278.svg)\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/23/23d7804912a10685756bb4ec89d12e3f6659b435ebe4d22c436684fb6eef9df8.svg)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)=y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)=x, \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2})=0,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})=2y, \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{y}{x},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n![](images/ea/eae5dd91db0cc17bec3af0a4781c7a58e8c65edb6c4139642f9c806dfc9c2473.svg)\nThus, the missing entries are $y,\\:$ $0,\\:$ and $\\ln{x}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198055 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198413 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e90349c-33ae-5b60-9d0d-7542dfebbbc7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Plane to the Graph of a Scalar Function in Matrix Form"}] :question/id #uuid "db0be438-94fe-52de-9daf-60d1727ce7b9" :question/math-academy-id "e-12479" :question/problem "Consider the function $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R,$ defined as\n\n![](images/7c/7c033fac25d53675fc47eaf5184d33f95c6e3524d82a4c547fb900e13c5205f7.svg)\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at ![](images/9c/9c89326030e3216291d4c0fbd715cdd634a567b6dae3f0d26620d19ee5f470ea.svg) is given by\n\n![](images/62/62ad44fc4734bc7006e9cc72a3e142d2df1d932f3fcdf1c0c6c125e8bd19d626.svg)\n\nfind the missing values." :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by \n\n![](images/82/826e5972b8b35e46f759dfb8f465316db48e36b12ef37d73b7bce6931f65a3d9.svg) \n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x},$ then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{x},$ denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}),$ is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n![](images/85/85c62ebacb5d1aaf052165a76d44b42dc83a2918020320326fd4bbe8cc4da14c.svg)\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/93/93faf7b5ccbadf7600ec47c7e63cac088211130f0f30109fbc71f2d7081139b9.svg)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{e}^{y})=3{x}^{2},\\, & \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{e}^{y})={e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n![](images/65/65e55a996288ffb34ef5b44b9b49dc7adaf681d379625733e7e74f8363f3f36f.svg)\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at ![](images/6a/6a0a72041eadc608c5f2ef7217a5e627115cee3bce332751a5cb46acd9dc0c24.svg) we have\n\n![](images/ee/ee17f93ff906242db59f3ef934b9501e7335a46ba5710e3fd89645e83be5ba34.svg)\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz - f(\\mathbf a) = \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(\\mathbf a),$ $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a),$ and $\\mathbf a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n![](images/42/42b3574762b1d9b14d27f8ba29b5c89bca66ec03724fbec7557c7bf6e5524972.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198056 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198414 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f09fc28-bdce-554f-98f5-019d34542a13" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Affine Approximation of a Vector Function"}] :question/id #uuid "f3540571-9159-50b4-a46c-4cf28a454602" :question/math-academy-id "e-12484" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^2,$ defined as\n\n![](images/f8/f8eb8b775c1645d73acd7313f54bdff4cdb55830ac6ebea2165c9263321ffb9e.svg)\n\nIf the linear approximation of $\\boldsymbol f$ at ![](images/18/18b82def6f36d90157e99ef72f14b857cd1e0c3007e684c660c31fd61f805040.svg) is given by\n\n![](images/91/91e5867a6be8302649edc9c2e1e4c4642c60853c7c3ad9bb2d25f9c649b5889d.svg)\n\nwhat are the missing values?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by \n\n![](images/43/43ffd01ab7a6d78ea0343953b985d9c4165ee76085f345bcd500c053550e3ec9.svg) \n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x},$ then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{x},$ denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}),$ is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n![](images/51/51191f4e8b1417de7b391a71d68c7c5917ed7083cc3b7a630662d6a0912b969c.svg)\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/06/066be34bb8bd7f585bc3d2666007e189f6c82f2e3b7f7a699928066825fd765d.svg)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{1})={x}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}_{1},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{1})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{2})=0,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{2})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n![](images/86/869b2f5de537961c4c83523ac6fcfd96329fc062e34509d88b8516b7a2259b84.svg)\n\nEvaluating $\\boldsymbol{f}$ and its derivative at ![](images/b3/b35d7585ed24aea694c3a31e69c1406744986db30cddf8842a5bae8d6d745e4b.svg) we have\n\n![](images/9f/9f3dbb19be577a74b8f19fd1eb5d42910fd0e46637388a3daab80c17d6edd833.svg)\n\nThe linear approximation of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}\\left(\\mathbf x \\right) \\approx \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf a) + \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf a),$ $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a),$ and $\\mathbf a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n![](images/5d/5d526b2485b53b029bc119e66e6a385d0ba228ecca1ffd0c58e7934e83a97a7b.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198633 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198412 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a818e04-786a-56b6-90cf-dcb573ccfa64" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Vector Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52ae776e-08b3-54b7-a885-e9217261d1b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}^{2}y,\\,$ $\\frac{1}{z},\\,$ and ${x}^{2}y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427508} {:answer/id #uuid "595978fe-cf25-5127-9b94-47cb53657dd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}^{2}y,\\,$ $\\frac{1}{x},\\,$ and ${x}^{2}y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427509} {:answer/id #uuid "b28b947e-e099-5a84-a1f1-e49c76339f4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2xyz,\\,$ $\\frac{1}{y},\\,$ and $yz$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427513} {:answer/id #uuid "ce8a1cde-3900-5ec2-8cba-bd78da711720" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2xyz,\\,$ $\\frac{1}{z},\\,$ and ${y}^{2}z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427514} {:answer/id #uuid "f3204833-987c-5155-a299-7bcc45208ed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2xyz,\\,$ $\\frac{1}{y},\\,$ and ${x}^{2}y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427516}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3204833-987c-5155-a299-7bcc45208ed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2xyz,\\,$ $\\frac{1}{y},\\,$ and ${x}^{2}y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427516} :answer-field/id #uuid "d21567a5-5f65-5300-b094-9bc5b4d4fdfd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d8b4c68b-51c3-543e-a36a-3adc1afaee33" :question/math-academy-id "q-162942" :question/problem "For the transformation $f:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz) \\\\ {x}^{2}yz\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe total derivative ${f}^{′}$ is given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x} & \\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} & \\frac{1}{z} \\\\ \\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} & {x}^{2}z & \\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing entries?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂z}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))=\\frac{1}{x},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))=\\frac{1}{y},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))=\\frac{1}{z} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz)=2xyz,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}yz)={x}^{2}z,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}yz)={x}^{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x} & \\frac{1}{y} & \\frac{1}{z} \\\\ 2xyz & {x}^{2}z & {x}^{2}y\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427518 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198411 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d912b296-6fd0-57c2-84e8-ca0b56549756" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Scalar Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43e47057-a760-57db-a3bf-60ce0300791b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{xy} & x{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427507} {:answer/id #uuid "87f84105-a6a8-5b4c-a4a3-205cfc0c3ca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & {e}^{y}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427510} {:answer/id #uuid "9d3b2b1d-700e-5b18-891c-7521d97ad052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{x} & x{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427511} {:answer/id #uuid "b0cb1f9c-4d73-5f31-bf65-3a70242b80f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y} \\\\ {e}^{x}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427512} {:answer/id #uuid "d60b9746-1d9b-5db9-b9ad-a63af5daaf53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{y} \\\\ x{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43e47057-a760-57db-a3bf-60ce0300791b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{xy} & x{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427507} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fb0a0b4-de3e-5b7b-8384-6969878cf78c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f77fb21a-c8bd-5017-9b85-73935660c6a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-163034" :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={e}^{xy},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\ncalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xy})=y{e}^{xy}, & \\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy})=x{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{xy} & x{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367762 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198411 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d912b296-6fd0-57c2-84e8-ca0b56549756" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Scalar Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11b80b86-64bb-54d9-a6ca-b27a6eaebca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367764} {:answer/id #uuid "3c49bd99-3471-511b-957a-8ab3b1903bc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367765} {:answer/id #uuid "94531792-a246-55d0-95b4-9f8194bd82a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367766} {:answer/id #uuid "715f6f02-e6fb-5ed3-ad0c-c279c3b1e987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367767} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b636fa-6aba-526b-9de7-3981175973af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11b80b86-64bb-54d9-a6ca-b27a6eaebca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367764} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f8006a8-9366-5d6f-ae22-58b3ac1b10e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b8b2963e-d3c7-59db-be98-15648e5aba5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-163048" :question/problem "For the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=x{y}^{2}-y,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\ncalculate ${f}^{′}$ at $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-y)={y}^{2}, & \\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-y)=2xy-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & 2xy-1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand ${f}^{′}$ at $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)=[\\begin{aligned}(1)^{2} & 2(0)(1)-1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198653 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198413 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e90349c-33ae-5b60-9d0d-7542dfebbbc7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Plane to the Graph of a Scalar Function in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f0af6f4-8846-5cc9-b0d0-ef675db7ecfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194550} {:answer/id #uuid "93e51430-7515-5ea7-b676-8718aa40b834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194551} {:answer/id #uuid "f248937d-3d19-5f99-a0d4-d58ced4eba66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194552} {:answer/id #uuid "15e93809-1fc8-5035-b5b6-cb4d0ca11d45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194553} {:answer/id #uuid "263c53e8-2f06-5be5-9f6a-884b62308d77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15e93809-1fc8-5035-b5b6-cb4d0ca11d45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194553} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbabfec0-d273-5628-a4d6-fe7beb8d8851" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1286acb9-42cc-5da3-a992-0e5c7b3d2c79" :question/math-academy-id "q-163057" :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={e}^{x}+y,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-∗=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}+y)={e}^{x},\\, & \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}+y)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=2,\\,{f}^{′}(a)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-f(a)={f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nz-2=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n2+1+1=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198413 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e90349c-33ae-5b60-9d0d-7542dfebbbc7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Plane to the Graph of a Scalar Function in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fa2eb46-7da9-5a93-ab46-905177cc8d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367792} {:answer/id #uuid "20e67f4c-0912-57d9-b100-79cfe229487b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367793} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d766a7-f722-54d5-a4bd-8afd2c4dbcfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367794} {:answer/id #uuid "c70eb82d-5858-5ea5-af10-6241b5f04e4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367795} {:answer/id #uuid "2e4cae0c-20a3-51e2-90c3-75d418d9f057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c70eb82d-5858-5ea5-af10-6241b5f04e4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367795} :answer-field/id #uuid "1db7e5b9-cbc0-5e13-8675-3951d7667a88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "08e9a1c4-09e3-536e-9e2b-e51736b93226" :question/math-academy-id "q-163074" :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{xy+1},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-∗=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{xy+1})=-\\frac{y}{(xy+1)^{2}},\\, & \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{xy+1})=-\\frac{x}{(xy+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{(xy+1)^{2}} & -\\frac{x}{(xy+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,{f}^{′}(a)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-f(a)={f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nz-\\frac{1}{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{1}{4})+(-\\frac{1}{4})=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367797 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198414 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f09fc28-bdce-554f-98f5-019d34542a13" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Affine Approximation of a Vector Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d2eba0c-14d4-56fe-8ab5-619751969660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367799} {:answer/id #uuid "7503905e-9b4f-5407-8c01-413a7b68830f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367800} {:answer/id #uuid "21f31c1f-b694-556a-98db-778605e79743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367801} {:answer/id #uuid "8433daa5-6e2c-55ec-b2e3-edef5e21a44a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367802} {:answer/id #uuid "f01146e8-4036-5342-a70e-9a2652f1ab42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d2eba0c-14d4-56fe-8ab5-619751969660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367799} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a050f39-aa59-5d92-be17-99b992f19596" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "89e6f405-4017-5664-a468-c2ee8d215a47" :question/math-academy-id "q-163083" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf the linear approximation of $f$ at  $a=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4} \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & -1\\end{aligned}](x-[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}]),\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}^{2})=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}^{2})=2{x}_{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2{x}_{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{f}^{′}(a)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\pi  \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe linear approximation of $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nf(x)≈[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\pi  \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}](x-[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}]).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n0+\\pi +(-1)=\\pi -1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198412 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a818e04-786a-56b6-90cf-dcb573ccfa64" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Vector Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d959d5d7-cbc1-5318-b797-c37a0afbbf37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x{e}^{xyz},\\,$ $z,\\,$ and $z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401414} {:answer/id #uuid "b30dc2cc-68a4-5912-8a3e-df5e08613c6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${e}^{xyz},\\,$ $2x,\\,$ and $y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401415} {:answer/id #uuid "3fd45803-73b4-58a2-b054-1d9f41e88d38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$yz{e}^{yz},\\,$ $0,\\,$ and $y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401416} {:answer/id #uuid "43123342-28a0-58f0-8627-2ed0371a4ca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$yz{e}^{xyz},\\,$ $z,\\,$ and $y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401417} {:answer/id #uuid "1c151508-a070-5be8-9646-29a3b6c0d0e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z{e}^{xyz},\\,$ $z,\\,$ and $z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43123342-28a0-58f0-8627-2ed0371a4ca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$yz{e}^{xyz},\\,$ $z,\\,$ and $y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401417} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cbe7815-5a90-5779-9948-27720bbf467a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e4436f3f-18fe-5775-815d-04fe166d17a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-163100" :question/problem "For the transformation $f:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{xyz} \\\\ x+yz\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe total derivative ${f}^{′}$ is given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} & xz{e}^{xyz} & xy{e}^{xyz} \\\\ 1 & \\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} & \\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing entries?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂z}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xyz})=yz{e}^{xyz},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xyz})=xz{e}^{xyz},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{xyz})=xy{e}^{xyz} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+yz)=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+yz)=z,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+yz)=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}yz{e}^{xyz} & xz{e}^{xyz} & xy{e}^{xyz} \\\\ 1 & z & y\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198414 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f09fc28-bdce-554f-98f5-019d34542a13" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Affine Approximation of a Vector Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bc8f2d1-ecfb-50d7-b14b-04f9786d82ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367834} {:answer/id #uuid "5f7d0746-3536-5992-9206-08d262d180df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367835} {:answer/id #uuid "64ad6963-dfe5-5f16-bbef-d203883d7998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367836} {:answer/id #uuid "566980c0-9d00-5a29-b05a-cac367edfbb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367837} {:answer/id #uuid "dc697d16-cd1d-50e1-81a4-0a7ea90187b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64ad6963-dfe5-5f16-bbef-d203883d7998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367836} :answer-field/id #uuid "703aea1e-c64a-581f-b4f6-a53c00907653" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b78101c4-fecc-5cba-9eee-2c628a3b3929" :question/math-academy-id "q-163162" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{3}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}}\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf the linear approximation of $f$ at $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈[\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}](x-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]),\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}^{3}-{x}_{2})=3{x}_{1}^{2},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}^{3}-{x}_{2})=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}})={x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}})={e}^{{x}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}^{2} & -1 \\\\ {x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}} & {e}^{{x}_{1}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{f}^{′}(a)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe linear approximation of $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nf(x)≈[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}](x-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n1+(-1)+1=1.\n$$"}]}
